A||||35||Hartz||3.1L V6 questions||||||
Z||000000||Hartz||07-26-99||03:29 AM||||For those of you who have rebuilt your stock V6 into a 3.1L, can anyone point me to more information regarding this buildup?  Is there a FAQ or webpage somewhere that details this?   What kinds of compression ratios are resultant from this rebuild?  Does it require colder plugs?  What kind of timing settings are being used?<p>Any thoughts or tip appreciated.<p>Thanks in advance,<br>Hartz<br>
Z||000001||FieroMike||07-26-99||06:42 AM||||Hartz,<br>In my experience all my local machine shops were pretty unhelpful with this modification.  I was all set out to have an eazy install, but they didn't know that a 2.8 could become a 3.1 or what parts to use and pretty much was giving me a hard time for even having a Fiero.  That is just my experience.  Here is a link that all Fiero enthusiast's should have, look under "Performance for the V-6".<p> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/biz/fierosails/info.html</A> 
Z||000002||Hartz||07-26-99||10:01 AM||||FieroMike,<p>Wow.  I've been out of the Fiero games for quite a while now.  That is some list of links.  Guess that should keep me busy for a while.  :-)<p>Basically, I've got a 2.8L V6 that was built up as a 3.1L turbo.  It's got the 3.1L crank, custom pistons, and all kinds of machine work and go-fast goodies.  Sadly, it's never run "quite right."  I set it aside a couple of years ago, due to a combination of frustration and lack of time.  Interest is starting to return, and I am debating with myself the best course of action:  Rip the turbo off and attempt to return it to a nicely built up naturally aspirated motor, or continue to try and debug the turbo setup.<p>The forged pistons that are in it now give it a compression ratio of 8.57:1.  My only fear with going naturally aspirated is that this will be a bit tame.<p>Anyway, thanks again for the links.<p>-Hartz<br>
Z||000003||batboy||07-26-99||10:10 AM||||A compression ratio of 8.5 to 8.6 to 1 is not all that tame nowadays considering the low octane grades of pump gas available. I'm guessing there are about a dozen people reading this that are drooling over that turbo set-up. I'd keep trying to work out the bugs, but if you decide not too, I'm sure you'll have no problems selling it. I'd use the spark plugs and timing recommendations for whatever engine the heads came off of as a starting point (I'm assuming they are the 2.8 HO Fiero heads).
Z||000004||FieroMike||07-26-99||12:24 PM||||Hartz,<br>If you have the time, I would try to get the Turbo fixed.  After being a loyal Fiero fan, I have to say that I am trading mine in for a '95 Camaro.  The one I am looking at is 11,500 - 2,000 down, you get a nice looking car, 305 HP v-8 and a six speed.  That is some bang for the buck.  If you already have the turbo in there, get it to work!! That baby will hall.  I spent 3,000 to get mine where it is, and another 2-3 for turbo, and 1 g on the paint and I about up to a '95 camaro.  Have fun!
Z||000005||Hartz||07-26-99||12:55 PM||||Thanks for the encouragement.  I suppose you guys have a point, but after so much money, and so much effort, it is getting frustrating.  After the heat from the turbo melted my ignition wires into a little ball of goo, I had to set it aside for a while and recover.  She's back running again, but it just isn't where it ought to be.  Something tells me that taking the turbo off will make things easier to get sorted.  Anyway, just to share, here's what we've got so far:<p>Parts List:<br>* ARP Head and Main studs, washers, nuts, rod bolts, and nuts.<br>* Accel 8.8 Spiral Core plug wires.<br>* MSD 6BTM Ignition (Boost Timing Master version of 6AL)<br>* Accel Super Coil<br>* JE Pistons, forged aluminum (8.57:1 CR), tool steel pins, locks, and rings.<br>* Crane Compucam, lifters, valve springs, etc.<br>* 3.1 Liter Crankshaft (General Motors)<br>* Cloyes True Roller Timing set<br>* TH125-C Automatic Transmission shift kit<br>* Custom 2 1/2" mandral-bent exhaust system<br>* Supertrapp Stainless Steel 4A Muffer system <p>Machine Work:<br>* Balance and Blueprint<br>* Crossdrill crank, straighten and polish.<br>* Valve job - full radius, hand lapped and blended<br>* Resurface Heads<br>* Cut Decks<br>* Float Rods<br>* Bore and Hone with Deck Plate<br>* Port and polish cylinder heads<br>* Port Match exhaust manifolds<br>* Port Match Intake Manifold and Runners<br>* Polish side beams on rods<br>* Fabricate custom air intake to replace stock air inlet<p>FieroMike - F-bodies are my other love.  I just sold my '94 Z28.  (Got a '99 Firehawk)  I loved it.  They are awesome cars, with great aftermarket support.  Not as unique as Fieros, but a lot of fun.  Good luck.<p>-Hartz
Z||000006||Eric||07-26-99||08:46 PM||||I'm a little worried about the 3.1 too.  I've got my engine half torn down and I am still not sure how I want to build it up.  The worst part about the 3.1 is not being able to get higher compression forged pistons.  In fact, I don't think you can get forged pistons at all anymore.  Another problem is with my application.  I want to be able to rev the motor and I don't think the larger crank will hold the kind of rpms I need for autocrossing (for very long).  I've heard the 2.8 is a destroked version of the 3.1 and was used in "high performance" applications (seems like minivans have the 3.1 market all wrapped up).  I like the idea of increasing the displacement in torquey V8s, but in little V6s, I'd have to disagree.  Besides, most of the power a new camshaft would offer is in the upper rpm band.  Wouldn't it be a lot nicer knowing your engine was build from the bottom to top end to rev?  <p>Sorry Hartz if this is a little off topic
Z||000007||Hartz||07-27-99||02:45 AM||||Eric,<p>Check with JE for forged pistons.  They won't be cheap, but that's where I got mine.<p>If you're autocrossing, wouldn't you want good torque to come out of tight corners?  If so, then the extra stroke the 3.1L crank provides might be beneficial?  Now who's off topic?  :-)<p>-Hartz
Z||000008||FieroMike||07-27-99||07:12 AM||||Hartz,<br>I think you are right.  I think I will keep the Fiero at least, because I had a lot of fun it last night.  Which was also the first night I have driven it in 3 months!!  I was just mad.  Still want the LT1,LS1 though.  You ever get on ls1.com??  Later
Z||000009||Hartz||07-27-99||08:47 AM||||FieroMike,<p>Yeah, I've been known to lurk at ls1.com.  I also hang out at the SLP message board, and the F-Body message board.  <p>Both cars are great.  I consider myself fortunate to be able to have one of each.  My entire Fiero engine rebuild started because I wanted my "toy" car (the Fiero) to be faster than my daily driver (was the Camaro).  :-)<p>You rarely see nice Fieros these days, but Camaros are on every other street corner.  That has its good and bad points, as I'm sure you know.  Hang in there!<p>Hartz
Z||000010||Shaun41178||07-27-99||08:53 AM||||FieroMike: Actually the 95 Camaros came with the Lt-1 which was rated at 275 hp from the factory.  Unless there are a good amount of bolt-ons your V8 does not have 305 hp.  The corvettes in 95 were rated at 300 hp.  Now the LS-1's are rated at 305 hp but they cam out in 98.  The Corvette LS1 is rated at 345.<p>I bet that turbo 3.1 when running and tuned properly will have at least 275 hp.  Plus it will be in a car several hundred pounds lighter.  Can you say F-bod killer?
Z||000011||batboy||07-27-99||06:01 PM||||Eric, I don't mean to nitpick, but the 3.1 is actually a stroked 2.8, not the other way around. If you are or going to race it, there are ways to get it to rev higher, but it's not cheap. Just remember the higher you rev, the less amount of time it will last before you need to rebuild again. The secret is in the valve train. You have to use roller cam, roller lifters, true roller rockers (not just the roller tip ones), heavy duty double or triple valve springs, and light weight racing valves. Oh yeah, don't forget to use screw-in rocker studs.
Z||000012||lowCG||07-28-99||01:07 AM||||Sounds like you need to be able and "dial in" the stuff you've got.<br>There are a couple of good companies that make computer controls that would replace the ecm,and let you take over inputing the ignition,fuel,boost,(nitrous), for around $900.<br>I don't think I could tolerate an Fbody that was heavier,higher,with no baffles in the gas tank,very long,would probably just do an epic burnout and leave it somewhere,like a used car lot.
Z||000013||Eric||07-29-99||04:36 PM||||Dammit!  I can't find the forged pistons for the 2.8.  Does anybody know if TRW still makes them?  I got my engine skattered all over my garage and still don't know how I want to put it back together.  I think I might just do the 3.1 thing.  The pistons, however, are only hyperutectic, but I can get them for less than $100 a set.  Wonder how much power these can handle.  If I do decide to use the 3.1 crank, what are the balancing procedures?  I've been confused with this from day one (one motor is internally balanced and one is externally balanced).  I will also need a new flywheel, won't I?
Z||000014||RICK||07-29-99||04:47 PM||||Eric, try The Fiero Store (fierostore.com) that's where I got my TRW forged pistons (40 over)<br>
Z||000015||houlster||08-06-99||04:59 PM||||In answer to wanting a high rpm autocrosser, how about switching to a 3.4 block but using the 2.8 crank?  That should give you the same high rpm you can get with the 2.8, but still have somewhere around 3.0 - 3.1 total displacement from the larger bore.
Z||000016||fastonpumpgas||08-24-99||09:40 AM||||I am in the middle of my 85 2.8 to 3.1 conversion ... I was wondering what 3.1 parts I need and I am worried abou tthe balance. , Can anyone help me so I dont shake my motor to death  ...  thanks
Z||000017||Eric||08-24-99||07:04 PM||||There are a few roads you can go down when go to a 3.1.  First you have to decide how much money you want to spend.  Then you need to find the best parts within your budget(a big problem with my rebuild).  I wouldn't call mine an economy rebuild, but I don't want to spend much over 2 grand for everything.  There are a number of problems facing you, the biggest of which is finding a machinist who is willing to work on a motor with two cylinders lopped off.  Once you find this machinist, you will need to get him off his butt and look for some parts.  I don't know if you need to be concerned with balancing because I don't know the year of your Fiero.  Some later model Fieros, 88s for sure and maybe 87s, balanced the same as the 3.1s, so you wouldn't need a new flywheel.  Internal parts are a piece of cake except for the pistons.  There aren't too many high performace options out there.  You will have a hard time finding a set of forged pistons for under $700, but hypereutectics and cast are more plentiful.  I was going to use hyperetectics myself, but was warned that these were not the way to go.  They are prone to losing their tops (Oh dear!).  I think I will go with the stock cast replacements.  According to Chevy Power performance book (which I recommend finding), The stock pistons can handle a moderate perfomace engine(200 horsepower seems moderate).  There are a number of other tricks in that book.  If you need any help you can email me.  I'd be happy to answer any questions about the rebuild.
Z||000018||BOSTITCH||08-24-99||08:39 PM||||So Eric, You've done the 2.8 to 3.1 conv. (I to am in the middle of it) so I'd like to know some of the pros and cons of doing this. Do you have a more eratic idle than you started with? And I wanted to verify that cast pistons are okay to use.
Z||000019||Stroker||08-24-99||09:45 PM||||OK Guys,<p>First of all,you guys are making this 2.8-3.1 rebuild sound like a hard thing. IT'S NOT!!!<p> It is one of the EASIEST upgrade motors that you can build.It's just a matter of KNOWING which parts you need to assemble to build this torquey dude.<br> <br>Not to brag by any means, but why do you think they call me Stroker?<br>This engine rebuild can be done for a surprisingly low budget IF you do your homework.And this is with all new parts and the best ones at that!<br> <br> I built the one that is currently powering my 86 SE for under $1,000.<br> In fact, the bottom end(resized rods,3.1 crank(981 casting),pistons(hypereutectic-and NO THE TOPS DON'T COME OFF!!!!!!!!),rings(chrome),bearings(Clevite-con rods,cam,mains),and things like an adjustable fuel pressure regulator,flywheel from an 88 V6(Fiero,of course),timing chain and gears(and damper),and anything I may have forgotten to mention cost me around $700-believe it....or not!<p> Keep in mind that while doing this rebuild that I wanted to perfect this engine combination and put together a kit of parts that would be top notch.So I spent 3 months researching this rebuild and the possible combination of parts with the lowest possible cost and highest quality...PERIOD!!!<p> I talked to EVERYBODY and ANYBODY who was an authority on this upgrade and FINNALY decided on it instead of a V8!<p> I'm not saying I have all the answers,but after all that I've learned I sure can answer most of your questions on this upgrade.<p> It is one of the best options for power in our little gems and it will rev virtually the same as a 2.8!!(keep in mind that the ECM limits this to around a little over 6,000)<br> I am TOTALLY pleased with what it will do, and in a little while mine's going TURBO!<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><p>Sorry so long, but I had to say it.<br>YOU GUYS HAVE FUN!!!!!!!!<IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15><br> <br>        Stroker
Z||000020||Shaun41178||08-25-99||12:06 AM||||stroker: I have just started the purchasing process of my 3.2 rebuild but the only thing so far that I have purchased is the valvetrain and I am expecting to get everything wholesale at around $200(fingers crossed).  Could you provide a parts list of the pistons, rings and the all the bearings bearings and where you got them or just where you got them?  i am going to purchase the crank from autozone for $139 plus the core or is there anotehr place that I can get it cheaper?<p>Also I heard that i can get a new nuetral flexplate from Transtar for around $30? or I thought about just having the one I have now balanced for my new setup.<p>I have thought about going with all aRP studs for mains and heads and actually plan on buying these.<p>The pistons i thought of buying were hyper and am expecting them to be around $200 these are the federal mogul ones.  Are these the ones you used and if so how much did you pay?<p>I have my own parts list togethor (searching for a year for the best parts the best quality i could find) however I have no idea of the prices.  Any help would be appreciated.<p>Eric:  Who manufacturs these hyper pistons that you can get for under $100 and can I get these same pistons for that price or is this a special discount price to you?  Thanks
Z||000021||Shaun41178||08-25-99||12:07 AM||||stroker:  I forgot, if you would rather just email me you can at Shaun41178@aol.com  Thanks
Z||000022||Eric||08-25-99||08:36 AM||||The pistons are TRW or Speed Pro I don't remember which.  The price I was quoted came from Summit around $90 for the set.  I think I'm going to stick with standard cast because it sounds like they are more durable and a little lighter than the hypereutectics.  Durability is my main concern, so for the majority of the parts I will use GM.  The car may not have 280 horsepower on 10lbs of boost, but at least it will last a long time.
Z||000023||Eric||08-25-99||04:07 PM||||I just talked with my machinist today.  He said that he won't build me a motor with hypereutectic pistons.  I wouldn't question this man because he has been in the business too long.  There is no reason to spend a thousand dollars on a rebuild just to have a piston let go, even if there is just a small chance of it doing so.  Forced induction sounds like a really bad idea with these pistons.  The fact that they are already high compression for a street engine coupled with the fact that even a weak (5-8lbs of boost) is extremly hard on internal components, the setup sounds very dangerous.
Z||000024||batboy||08-25-99||06:53 PM||||Hey guys, I think hypereutectic pistons are getting a bum rap here. First, a regular cast piston is not stronger than a hypereutectic piston. Obviously forged pistons are the strongest and the only type of piston I would recommend for use with forced induction. However, hypereutectic pistons were designed to produce a stronger piston. These hypereutectic pistons are actually a special type of cast piston able to withstand higher rpm and cylinder pressures of STREET performance engines. Again, these are not racing pistons, but are much stronger than regular cast slugs because more silicon is added to the aluminum alloy. This high silicon alloy increases strength and wear resistance and reduces thermal expansion.
Z||000025||lowCG||08-25-99||11:41 PM||||The hypereutectic pistons have a steel sleeve,or band cast into them for reinforcing purposes,yet maintain the cylinder to bore clearance of a cast piston.<br>This allows you to drive your car while it's cold,with forged pistons,the clearances are much greater,and piston slap(rattling sound)will occur while cold,until warmed up,and sized correctly with the expansion factor.<br>For a daily driver,the hypereutectics are great,forged are better for more serious applications of horsepower(ie.recreational,racing,or serious hotrods).
Z||000026||lowCG||08-25-99||11:44 PM||||Cast are pretty junky really.
Z||000027||Eric||08-26-99||07:20 PM||||What I have said so far about hypereutectic pistons are the opinions of my machinist.  I really don't know the first thing about them for myself.  I went to the local chevy dealer today and ordered my camshaft and crank.  I tried to order the cast pistons, but found out they were going to cost me $360 for the set.  That's a little high, needless to say, and leaves the federal mogul hypereutectics looking pretty good at only $90 a set.  I think nitrous and turbos are out of the question with anything other than forged pistons, but your opinions so far are appreciated and I hope I can convince my machinist to see things the same way.
Z||000028||lowCG||08-26-99||09:21 PM||||I would get someone who can use measuring tools,other than the machinist,just inspect the work,worth it in the long run,and he doesn't have to know about it if everything measures out good.It just really sucks to spend all that effort,time and money,then find out that one person screwed up their part of the deal.Happens all the time too.
Z||000029||terryk||08-27-99||08:20 AM||||Silvolite has 8.8:1 pistons for the 3.1L<p>I'll try to dig up the PN. You should call them and confirm it, but it says 3.1L iron head. Available in .040" over too.<p>
Z||000030||Eric||08-27-99||05:14 PM||||I'll assume that piston's compression ratio is based on the iron head's volume.  I think Silv-O-Lite is a better piston than TRW but I really don't know.  It kind of pisses me off to see the same parts for a chevy 350 costing half as much.  Sometimes it makes me rethink this whole rebuild deal.
Z||000031||Dale||08-27-99||07:16 PM||||Compresion is a combination of combustion chamber volume and piston configuration (domed,flat-top, dished).  As far as hyperetechtic pistons go they are a pressure cast piston.  They are aprox. 30% stronger than cast pistons and disapate heat a little better becouse of the density of the aluminum<p>As far as ballancing goes, I ballance every engine that I have ever built for any performance.  It will last longer and run smoother providing the work is good.<p>My 2 cents
